    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It does feel random, but it's less about the internet ignoring the update and more about the sheer scale of the network. While caching is the biggest culprit, other factors can contribute to the inconsistency, like regional DNS resolvers or ISP-level caching. Some Internet Service Providers (ISPs) maintain their own local caches that can sometimes be slower to update than others.\n\nIt's a messy, decentralized system, which is why you sometimes see a patchwork of old and new information. You might even see a small, localized outage that is affecting a specific set of resolvers, which could be a temporary glitch. In short, the variability is a natural byproduct of this distributed system.",
